course: everyday
lesson: 410
title: Strangers who want to chat privately
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In Roblox, a very friendly player started chatting to Ruby. He liked her outfit. He liked her building. Then he asked how old she was and which school she went to. Ruby felt flattered — someone was interested in her! — and also a small cold feeling in her tummy. She did not answer. She told her mum, and together they blocked and reported him. Personal details are like house keys: you do not hand them out online, however nice the person seems.
explain: People online can pretend to be anyone. Ollie's new online friend said he was also ten, loved the same games and supported the same team, which was suspiciously perfect. Ollie's mum explained that you cannot see who is really typing, and Ollie kept the friendship to in-game chat only. When a stranger offered Kai free Robux if he would chat on a different app, Kai's dad explained that real friends do not offer gifts to move you somewhere private. Gifts from strangers usually want something back. And when a stranger told Zara she was «the best player EVER», she said «thanks» and kept chatting only in the game: lots of flattery from a stranger can be a hook to win your trust.
explain: Some messages are big red flags. A player told Priya «don't tell your parents about me, they wouldn't understand». She felt special and uneasy at the same time, and told her mum that very evening; her mum said she had done exactly the right thing. Any secret someone asks you to keep from your parents is a reason to tell them. When a player asked Mia for a photo of herself, she left the game and told her dad, and they blocked and reported him. When Theo's online friend asked to meet at the park, his dad said no meeting online people without a parent, and they reported the chat.
explain: Little clues add up. Jayden's username was his full name and birthday, so his mum helped him change it to «PurpleTaco», which he now loves more than his real name. Noah told a player his house was near the big football stadium, which felt like just chatting, until his dad explained that small clues can help someone find you. And Leo keeps his parents' rules on a sticky note on his screen. His friends teased him for a day, then Kai copied the list for his own screen.
fact: Never tell people you only know online your real name, age, school or address.
fact: People online can pretend to be any age and anyone they like.
fact: A stranger who asks you to move to a private chat or another app is a danger sign.
fact: A stranger who asks you to keep a secret from your parents is a reason to tell your parents.
fact: Never send photos of yourself to people you do not know in real life.
fact: Never meet someone from online without a parent fully involved.
fact: Free gifts from strangers online usually want something back.
fact: A username should be made up and should not show your real name or birthday.
fact: Small details, like a nearby stadium or shop, can add up to where you live.
fact: Blocking stops someone contacting you, and reporting tells the game company.
fact: Telling a trusted grown-up is never getting into trouble; it is getting help.
exercise: What did the friendly Roblox player ask Ruby? | How old she was and which school she went to | Which colour she had picked for her outfit that day | What her favourite game was | Whether she liked building
exercise: How did Ruby feel when he asked? | Flattered, with a small cold feeling in her tummy | Angry and wanting to shout at him in capital letters | Bored, because he talked too much | Nothing, it was just a question
exercise: What did Ruby do? | Did not answer, told her mum, blocked and reported him | Told him only her school, not her age, to be really polite | Gave a fake age but a real school | Kept chatting so he would not be hurt
exercise: Why are personal details like house keys? | You do not hand them out to strangers | They are kept together on a ring | They open every game | You lose them easily down the back of the sofa
exercise: A stranger offers Kai free Robux to chat on another app. What is true? | Gifts to move you somewhere private are a danger sign | Free Robux is always a kind present from a generous player | It is fine if the app is popular | It is fine if he sends the Robux first
exercise: A player tells Priya «don't tell your parents about me». What is wise? | Tell her parents straight away | Keep it secret to be a loyal friend to him | Ask him why he wants it kept secret first | Tell only her best friend, nobody else
exercise: Ollie's online friend says he is also ten. What did Ollie's mum explain? | People online can pretend to be any age | Ten-year-olds never lie about their age online | Online friends are always real | He should ask for a photo to check
exercise: What did Mia do when a player asked for her photo? | Left the game, told her dad and reported him | Sent an old photo from when she was little instead | Sent a photo of her cat | Asked for his photo first
exercise: Theo's online friend asks to meet at the park. What is the rule? | Never meet without a parent fully involved | Meet only in the daytime | Meet if he brings a friend along with him | Meet if the park is busy
exercise: Why did Jayden change his username? | It showed his full name and birthday | It was too long to type into the game | His friends did not like the sound of it | The game made him change it after a year
exercise: Why was it unwise for Noah to mention the football stadium? | Small clues can help someone find where you live | Stadiums are secret places | The player supported a different team and got cross | It was boring to talk about
exercise: Zara gets a message saying she is the best player ever. What did she do? | Said «thanks» and kept chatting only in the game | Gave him her phone number so they could talk | Moved to a private chat | Sent him her photo as thanks
exercise: Why is too much flattery from a stranger worth noticing? | It can be a hook to win your trust | It always means he is a great friend to have | It means you really are the best | It is how game staff talk
exercise: What did Leo keep on a sticky note on his screen? | His parents' online safety rules | His password and username | His friends' home addresses | His high scores
exercise: Which is a kind of courage online? | Telling a parent even when a stranger said not to | Meeting an online friend alone | Keeping a secret to be loyal to a new online friend | Answering every question politely
exercise: Is telling a grown-up about a creepy message getting into trouble? | No, it is getting help, and it is right | Yes, you will lose the console for a month | Yes, you should have known | Only if you answered the message
exercise: What does blocking a player do? | It stops him contacting you | It deletes his account for good | It sends him a warning message | It tells his parents what he did
test: «some guy in Roblox asked what school i go to, he seems nice tho» | Don't answer, tell a grown-up, block and report | Tell him, he seems nice and he's a good builder | Tell him just the town | Ask him his school first
test: «he said FREE robux if we chat on another app!!! should i» | No, tell a grown-up, it's a danger sign | Yes, free is free | Yes, but only for ten minutes, then come back | Only if he sends half first
test: «he said don't tell ur parents, they won't get it». What now? | Tell your parents tonight | Keep it quiet, he trusts you | Tell him you'll keep the secret | Delete the game and tell nobody
test: Ollie: «he's def 10, he said so and he likes the same team». True? | Anyone online can pretend to be any age | Yes, he said it so it's true | Yes, nobody ever bothers to lie about age | Only adults lie online
test: «player wants a pic of me, says he'll send one back». Best move? | Don't send, leave, tell a grown-up | Send one without your face showing | Wait for him to send his photo first | Send one of your big sister instead
test: «my online mate wants to meet at the park sat, can i go?» | Not without a parent fully involved | Yes, parks are public | Yes, if you take a friend with you | Yes, in daylight only
test: username «JaydenSmith2016». What's the problem? | It shows his real name and birth year | It's too short and easy for others to copy | Numbers aren't allowed in usernames at all | It sounds boring and nobody will notice it
test: «i only said I live by the big stadium, that's nothing». Really? | Small clues can add up to where you live | It's nothing, loads and loads of people live there | Only the street name matters | Stadiums are on every street
test: «stranger said I'm the best player EVER, gonna DM him». Wise? | Say thanks, keep it in the game chat | DM him, he's clearly a nice person | Give him your number | Send him your skins
test: Leo's mates: «why've u got rules stuck on ur screen, baby». Best reply? | «Rules I can see are rules I remember» | «My mum glued it, I can't help it» | «It's my password, don't look» | «Fine, I'll rip it off»
test: «if I tell mum about the creepy message, will I get in trouble?» | No, telling is getting help, and it's right | Yes, so don't tell her anything | Only if you answered it | Yes, you'll lose your console for good
exam: Identify the information which a pupil ought to withhold from a person known to him solely through an online game. | The pupil's real name, age, school and address | The pupil's preferred colour for his character within the game | The name of the pupil's favourite game | The pupil's opinion of a particular level
exam: Whereas a participant has requested that a pupil conceal their acquaintance from the pupil's parents, select the response most consistent with established guidance. | The pupil ought to inform his parents without delay | The pupil ought to honour the request in good faith | The pupil ought first to seek the participant's reasons | The pupil ought to inform no person whatsoever
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an offer of gifts made by an unknown participant conditional upon conversation in another application? | It constitutes a recognised warning sign warranting adult intervention | It constitutes a customary and harmless act of online generosity | It constitutes an official promotion of the game operator | It is unobjectionable provided the gift is received first
exam: State the principle consistent with the established facts concerning a proposed meeting in person with an acquaintance made online. | No such meeting ought to take place without full parental involvement | Such a meeting is permissible in any public place, such as a park or a shopping centre | Such a meeting is permissible during daylight hours | Such a meeting is permissible if a friend attends
